Pipelined LEGv8 CPU

A 64-bit ARM (LEGv8) CPU, built from the ground up in structural, gate-level SystemVerilog: every wide datapath signal is wired from explicit and/or/ xor/not gates (max 4 inputs each, 50ps delay each), and every state-holding element is built from a single given D_FF primitive. This repo is the fourth and final stage of a four-project sequence:

Project	What it added
1 — Register File	32×64-bit register file, gate-level, structural
2 — ALU	64-bit ALU: add, sub, and, or, xor, plus flags
3 — Single-Cycle CPU	Full LEGv8 datapath, one instruction per clock
4 — Pipelined CPU	5-stage pipeline, forwarding, branch resolution (this repo)
What the CPU does

Implements the LEGv8 instruction set: ADDI, ADDS, SUBS, AND, ORR, EOR, LDUR, STUR, B, B.LT, BL, BR, CBZ. It fetches, decodes, executes, accesses memory, and writes back one instruction per clock cycle in steady state, five instructions in flight simultaneously.

Architecture: 5 pipeline stages
IF  ->  ID  ->  EX  ->  MEM  ->  WB
fetch   decode  ALU    memory   register
                        access   write-back

Each stage is separated by a pipeline register (a bank of D_FFs, built via the reusable PipeReg module), which latches everything the next stage needs so a new instruction can enter the pipeline every single cycle.

Branch resolution happens in ID, one stage earlier than a "textbook" design. B, B.LT, BL, BR, and CBZ are all fully decided by the end of the Decode stage — using forwarded register/flag values (see below) — so the very next fetch after a branch is already correctly redirected. This means the design needs no flush or squash logic anywhere: the one guaranteed delay-slot instruction (per the course's ISA rules) is always the correct next instruction to execute, with no extra "shadow" fetch to throw away.

Hazards and how they're handled

Pipelining creates a real problem that doesn't exist in a single-cycle design: an instruction can need a value that a still-in-flight earlier instruction hasn't officially saved to the register file yet.

Data forwarding

ForwardSelect (one instance per register-file read port) checks three candidate sources every cycle — whatever's currently in EX, MEM, and WB — against the register number ID is about to read:

EX tap — the immediately preceding instruction (0 cycles of gap)
MEM tap — two instructions back
WB tap — three instructions back (the exact cycle a register is being both read in ID and written in WB simultaneously; the register file's read is purely combinational and can't see a same-edge write, so this tap exists specifically to close that gap)

RegMatch does the register-number comparison (5-bit XNOR-then-AND, same technique the ALU itself uses internally). IsReg31 excludes any candidate whose destination is X31 — a "write" there is architecturally a no-op, and since forwarding bypasses the register file entirely, an unguarded forward would hand a consumer the wrong nonzero value. ForwardMux then picks the freshest applicable candidate (EX > MEM > WB > raw register-file read).

The EX tap is explicitly disabled for a pending load (nMemRead_EX): a LDUR sitting in EX has only computed a memory address so far, not the loaded data (that arrives one stage later, in MEM). The course's mandatory one-instruction load delay slot guarantees no valid program ever needs this path — the gate exists as defense-in-depth, not as something the design relies on.

Flag forwarding

B.LT doesn't read a register — it reads the negative/overflow flags from the most recent ADDS/SUBS. Since SUBS can be immediately followed by B.LT (and the real course benchmarks do exactly this, back-to-back), a single 2:1 mux picks between the stored FlagsReg output and the live EX-stage ALU flags, selected by whether EX currently holds a flag-setting instruction. Same underlying idea as register forwarding, applied to a 2-bit resource instead of a 64-bit one.

What changed from the single-cycle CPU (Lab 3 → Lab 4)

Unchanged, byte-for-byte: regfile.sv, alu.sv, controlunit.sv, ImmGen.sv, datamem.sv, instructmem.sv, Mux2.sv, Mux4.sv, FlagsReg.sv, IsZero64.sv. Pipelining changes when a value is used, not what any single combinational block computes.

New files, all specific to pipelining:

File	Purpose
PipeReg.sv	Parameterized bank of D_FFs — the pipeline register building block
RegMatch.sv	5-bit register-number equality check
IsReg31.sv	Detects a destination of X31
ForwardMux.sv	Priority mux: raw value vs. up to 3 forwarded candidates
ForwardSelect.sv	Wires RegMatch+IsReg31 into a forwarding decision per read port

cpu.sv was restructured from one flat combinational block into five labeled sections joined by PipeReg instances, with the forwarding network inserted right after the register file's read outputs — everything downstream (the ALU, the branch-decision logic, STUR's store-data) reads the forwarded values, never the register file's raw output directly.

A real bug found and fixed along the way

Testing against the recursive test12_Fibonacci benchmark exposed that BR's target register is encoded in the Rd/Rt field (instruction[4:0]), not the Rn field (instruction[9:5]) every other instruction uses. BR now correctly reads its target via the same ReadRegister2/ReadReg2Src path already used for STUR's store-data and CBZ's zero-check register.

Verification

Tested against all 9 provided course benchmarks — every final register and memory value checked against each file's own documented expected results (several of which explicitly differ between single-cycle and pipelined execution, e.g. delay-slot counters):

Benchmark	Exercises
test01_AddiB	ADDI, unconditional B
test02_AddsSubs	ADDS/SUBS chains, adjacent-instruction hazards
test03_CbzB	CBZ, multiple branch types, delay-slot counting
test04_LdurStur	LDUR/STUR round-trip
test05_Blt	Back-to-back SUBS→B.LT (flag forwarding)
test06_BlBr	BL/BR subroutine call and return
test10_forwarding	Purpose-built hazard stress test
test11_Sort	Bubble sort — sustained loop execution
test12_Fibonacci	Recursive BL/BR, deepest hazard coverage

All nine pass with zero assertion failures.

Building and running

Simulated with Icarus Verilog and viewed with GTKWave, driven by the included Makefile:

bash
make compile   # build sim.out from all sources
make run       # simulate; prints the same pass/fail trace as ModelSim
make wave      # open the resulting waveform in GTKWave
make clean     # remove build products

instructmem.sv's `define BENCHMARK line selects which .arm test program gets loaded; its path is resolved relative to wherever you invoke make/vvp from, so keep a benchmarks/ folder one level above this project's directory (or point the define at a same-directory filename instead).

Also runs in ModelSim via runlab.do / cpu_wave.do, which set up a wave window showing the PC, all 32 registers, data memory, flags, clock, and reset.
